What is the difference between compute intensive, memory intensive, and I.O. intensive applications?

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:06.280
Now I know these are things that we haven't talked about in detail.

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:11.480
So the way you can think about compute-intensive, memory-intensive, and IO-intensive,

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:15.579
if you can remember the conversation that we had last week,

00:50:15.700 --> 00:50:18.760
we talked about when it comes to size scalability,

00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:22.980
some of the bottlenecks that can appear have to do with the components of the computer.

00:50:23.660 --> 00:50:27.940
So we want to add more computers because, for example,

00:50:27.940 --> 00:50:31.220
our application depends on the performance of the CPU,

00:50:31.219 --> 00:50:36.179
right and we cannot buy a more powerful cpu so we add another computer with another powerful cpu

00:50:36.179 --> 00:50:43.099
or maybe the application depends more on how fast the memory system is right so in for example we

00:50:43.099 --> 00:50:48.059
can have applications that exhibit random memory access so they cannot benefit from the cpu cache

00:50:48.059 --> 00:50:53.359
we cannot you know bring the data closer to the cpu so then we are at the mercy of how fast the

00:50:53.359 --> 00:50:58.480
memory subsystem is or we might have application that process large amounts of data that cannot

00:50:58.480 --> 00:51:02.519
it into memory, so we need to read and write data a lot, so this means that the performance

00:51:02.519 --> 00:51:06.619
of the application depends on how fast we can read data from this, and if we can

00:51:06.619 --> 00:51:10.860
keep it in memory in an additional way. So, for each of these

00:51:10.860 --> 00:51:14.579
let's say components of a

00:51:14.579 --> 00:51:18.300
computer, you can have an application that has

00:51:18.300 --> 00:51:22.619
a bottleneck, that one of the components becomes a bottleneck for the application.

00:51:22.780 --> 00:51:26.340
So we say that an application is compute intensive, if

00:51:26.340 --> 00:51:31.260
the speed of the application depends on the CPU, on the speed of the CPU.

00:51:31.960 --> 00:51:37.220
So, right, you implement an application, and even for this performance evaluation,

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:41.140
know that when you do this performance evaluation, you will get some numbers.

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:45.700
Your numbers will probably be very different from the numbers of one of your colleagues

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:50.140
because, right, the performance will depend on the software implementation,

00:51:50.320 --> 00:51:53.360
but also on the hardware in which the software is running.

00:51:53.800 --> 00:51:55.500
So even if you have the same software implementation,

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:58.120
if you have different hardware, you're going to get different performance.

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:02.720
But, again, if an application, if the performance of the application

00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:05.079
depends on how fast the processor is running,

00:52:05.440 --> 00:52:07.220
that would be computer-intensive application.

00:52:07.220 --> 00:52:10.640
If an application depends on how fast the memory subsystem

00:52:10.640 --> 00:52:15.579
can bring data to the CPU,

00:52:15.960 --> 00:52:18.019
then it means it's a memory-intensive application.

00:52:18.240 --> 00:52:21.780
Typically, applications that exhibit random memory access,

00:52:22.059 --> 00:52:23.079
they are memory-intensive.

00:52:23.360 --> 00:52:32.099
And then lastly, IO-intensive applications are applications that depend on the speed of the hard drive or of the network, so the input-output devices.

00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:42.420
So this is usually applications that have large amounts of data that cannot fit into memory, usually become IO-intensive.

00:52:42.960 --> 00:52:46.880
Now it depends. If you're just reading the data once and processing things, that's fine.

