02:16:01.600 --> 02:16:06.680
So, when it comes to architectures, I just want to mention one thing.

02:16:06.680 --> 02:16:10.619
So the book calls them architectural styles.

02:16:10.619 --> 02:16:13.180
So we got to define what is an architecture.

02:16:13.180 --> 02:16:17.520
Basically, architecture is formulated as a set of components that have well defined

02:16:17.520 --> 02:16:22.660
interfaces and then we describe how are these components connected to each other, how data

02:16:22.660 --> 02:16:27.220
is exchanged between the components and then how the components and the connectors are

02:16:27.220 --> 02:16:30.380
are jointly configured into a system.

02:16:30.380 --> 02:16:32.780
So, we use the notion of a connector.

02:16:32.780 --> 02:16:36.059
So, the connector, it's a special kind of component

02:16:36.059 --> 02:16:39.440
that mediates communication, coordination, cooperation.

02:16:39.440 --> 02:16:42.739
We'll focus in this class more on the communication part,

02:16:42.739 --> 02:16:46.479
but we'll cover coordination as well.

02:16:46.479 --> 02:16:48.780
So, it can be implemented in various ways.

02:16:48.780 --> 02:16:51.399
But, we'll spend more time on the connector technology

02:16:51.399 --> 02:16:55.420
as well in chapter 4.

02:16:55.420 --> 02:17:04.819
So when it comes to architectures, we need to distinguish between two kinds of architectures.

02:17:05.239 --> 02:17:09.620
So I know we talked at the beginning in the introduction of some of the misconceptions.

02:17:10.180 --> 02:17:14.899
We said that some people would say that, oh, a centralized solution is not very great.

02:17:15.120 --> 02:17:19.059
But centralized can mean either logically centralized or physically centralized.

02:17:19.620 --> 02:17:21.620
Physically centralized means you have one computer.

02:17:22.400 --> 02:17:25.060
logically means the service is logically one single

02:17:25.060 --> 02:17:27.820
service. But you can have a centralized

02:17:27.820 --> 02:17:30.900
system that is logically centralized but physically

02:17:30.900 --> 02:17:34.079
distributed. So when we talk about architectures

02:17:34.079 --> 02:17:37.540
in this class, we'll talk about both logical architectures

02:17:37.540 --> 02:17:40.300
or software architectures and then physical or hardware

02:17:40.300 --> 02:17:43.940
in terms of how you organize a distributed

02:17:43.940 --> 02:17:46.100
system. So let's start first with

02:17:46.100 --> 02:17:49.540
logical architectures or software architectures.

02:17:50.120 --> 02:17:53.920
And the most popular architecture out there is, of course, the layered architecture.

02:17:54.060 --> 02:17:55.520
This is the easiest one to understand.

02:17:56.140 --> 02:18:02.860
And this architecture has been adopted in a wide range of applications, not just in computers.

02:18:04.600 --> 02:18:08.740
Layering is the name of a technique that can be used to break down complexity.

02:18:10.060 --> 02:18:13.960
This is how we even implement operating systems nowadays.

02:18:14.360 --> 02:18:16.540
They are implemented under the form of layers.

02:18:16.540 --> 02:18:21.580
You have the hardware layer at the bottom, then on top of it you have the kernel layer

02:18:21.580 --> 02:18:28.760
that manages the hardware, provides the process, virtual memory and file abstractions.

02:18:28.760 --> 02:18:33.460
On top of it you have some sort of shell or desktop user interface, where the shell could

02:18:33.460 --> 02:18:38.220
be a command line shell or it could be a graphical user interface that allows users to then interact

02:18:38.220 --> 02:18:39.680
with the resources of the computer.

02:18:39.680 --> 02:18:42.480
And then on top of that you have the applications.

02:18:42.480 --> 02:18:45.480
This is an example of a layered architecture.

02:18:45.479 --> 02:18:50.159
is also used in communication protocols and we'll talk more once we talk about

02:18:50.159 --> 02:18:56.799
communication distributed systems how the internet protocols are organized and

02:18:56.799 --> 02:19:02.520
what we can use to implement distributed applications now when it comes to

02:19:02.520 --> 02:19:07.659
layered architecture the most common form of architecture of layered

02:19:07.659 --> 02:19:12.319
architecture is the request response down call architecture so this

02:19:12.319 --> 02:19:14.539
This architecture implements multiple layers.

02:19:14.699 --> 02:19:18.579
Each layer is responsible with a certain part of the functionality of the application.

02:19:19.219 --> 02:19:23.699
And they have a strict hierarchy where the layer on top only contacts the layer on the bottom.

02:19:24.180 --> 02:19:26.399
And then that layer only contacts its own layer.

02:19:26.840 --> 02:19:31.619
And we have usually some sort of request that goes to the layer and then the response that comes back to the layers.

02:19:32.079 --> 02:19:36.279
But the layer on top does not contact a separate layer somewhere else.

02:19:36.279 --> 02:19:39.219
So this is very typical in computer networks.

02:19:39.340 --> 02:19:41.500
The communication protocol is following this approach.

02:19:42.319 --> 02:19:53.879
You can also have layered architectures called one-way call, layered architectures, where a layer can contact layers, not just the layer underneath, but also layers that sit multiple levers underneath.

02:19:54.459 --> 02:20:05.440
If you want to think of an example of an application that uses this, let's say that this layer on the bottom is the layer that implements the system calls of an operating system.

02:20:05.440 --> 02:20:22.300
And then layer n-1 rotates a library that implements, let's say, matrix multiplication and operations on a matrix, including storing the matrix on disk, which then means that that library needs to make calls to the kernel, needs to make calls to layer n-3.

02:20:22.779 --> 02:20:28.439
Let's say that layer n-1 is an application, then, that, you know, operates on matrices.

02:20:28.439 --> 02:20:36.219
So then can, you know, use the functions provided by layer n-1 by the library, but also does its own system calls to the operating system.

02:20:36.459 --> 02:20:36.519
Okay.

02:20:37.840 --> 02:20:39.359
It doesn't have to be too complicated.

02:20:39.899 --> 02:20:45.119
And then lastly, you can also have layered architectures where you support upcalls.

02:20:45.599 --> 02:20:50.840
So, for example, you have a layer that can either send a request to another layer,

02:20:50.840 --> 02:20:55.559
or you can register some sort of notify operation

02:20:55.559 --> 02:20:58.620
so that when layer n-2 has some sort of data,

02:20:58.760 --> 02:21:00.559
you can do an up call to the upper layer

02:21:00.559 --> 02:21:02.659
or you can send a signal to the upper layer

02:21:02.659 --> 02:21:04.739
to let it know that the data is available.

02:21:05.500 --> 02:21:08.020
This type of layer architecture is very popular

02:21:08.020 --> 02:21:09.979
when it comes to the implementation

02:21:09.979 --> 02:21:14.600
of input-output operations in operating systems, right?

02:21:14.600 --> 02:21:16.559
So if I were to ask you when it comes

02:21:16.559 --> 02:21:17.899
to operating systems, right,

02:21:17.899 --> 02:21:26.399
What are two ways that you can implement, let's say, the function of reading data from a file from disk?

02:21:26.739 --> 02:21:28.879
We know that the disk is very slow, right?

02:21:28.940 --> 02:21:33.879
So the program has two options here when it's waiting for the data that comes from the disk.

02:21:34.000 --> 02:21:36.680
Do you happen to know what those two strategies are called?

02:21:39.779 --> 02:21:41.159
Polling and interrupt, right?

02:21:41.479 --> 02:21:46.659
So polling means that the program will stay awake and will try to keep on asking the operating system,

02:21:46.659 --> 02:21:49.399
hey, is the data here yet? Is the data here yet? Is the data here yet?

02:21:49.719 --> 02:21:55.000
Which means that it might use CPU time while being scheduled by the operating system.

02:21:55.119 --> 02:21:59.959
So it might not be a very efficient, let's say, solution unless your device is very fast.

02:22:01.539 --> 02:22:06.799
So it's more common in supercomputer architecture where the network devices are actually pretty fast.

02:22:08.360 --> 02:22:12.159
Or you can do interrupts, right, where the process tells the operating system,

02:22:12.159 --> 02:22:15.440
hey, I want that data from there, and then the process goes to sleep,

02:22:15.440 --> 02:22:18.860
and then the operating system sends a signal to the process to wake it up.

02:22:19.159 --> 02:22:21.760
This is the equivalent of that up call in a sense

02:22:21.760 --> 02:22:24.200
where it sends a signal to the upper layer to let it know that,

02:22:24.420 --> 02:22:25.800
hey, things are already there.

02:22:29.720 --> 02:22:32.620
We'll talk more about communication protocols,

02:22:34.120 --> 02:22:36.739
what's important when it comes to communication protocols.

02:22:37.159 --> 02:22:37.899
Oops.

02:22:40.700 --> 02:22:42.800
When it comes to communication protocols,

02:22:42.799 --> 02:22:44.719
usually

02:22:44.719 --> 02:22:47.420
you hear the terms

02:22:47.420 --> 02:22:49.420
protocol, service and interface

02:22:49.420 --> 02:22:51.299
they have very specific

02:22:51.299 --> 02:22:53.619
meanings and we know

02:22:53.619 --> 02:22:55.279
that communication protocols between

02:22:55.279 --> 02:22:56.399
two entities

02:22:56.399 --> 02:22:59.500
are implemented into multiple

02:22:59.500 --> 02:23:01.420
layers so we say that each layer

02:23:01.420 --> 02:23:03.599
has its own interface that it provides

02:23:03.599 --> 02:23:05.279
to either application or

02:23:05.279 --> 02:23:06.959
layers as if above

02:23:06.959 --> 02:23:09.539
we say that

02:23:09.539 --> 02:23:11.859
a service

02:23:11.860 --> 02:23:19.560
represents the interface provided by a layer

02:23:19.560 --> 02:23:22.720
that sits at a specific level.

02:23:23.319 --> 02:23:26.640
And then a protocol refers to how the data is being transferred

02:23:26.640 --> 02:23:29.260
between the same layer between two parts.

02:23:29.800 --> 02:23:32.400
And then we say that a protocol stack

02:23:32.400 --> 02:23:35.520
is when you have a bunch of protocols

02:23:35.520 --> 02:23:38.440
that sit one on top of the other in a layered fashion.

02:23:38.659 --> 02:23:41.360
We'll talk more about communication protocol and communication.

02:23:41.360 --> 02:23:43.540
So we can, for now, skip it.

02:23:44.239 --> 02:23:46.739
And then when it comes to layered architecture,

02:23:46.960 --> 02:23:50.760
another very popular way to present, let's say, a layered architecture,

02:23:50.920 --> 02:23:52.480
especially in modern day applications,

02:23:52.640 --> 02:23:56.500
is, of course, the implementation of the application layering architecture.

02:23:56.920 --> 02:24:02.159
So here, as I mentioned, this is, again, a traditional pre-layered architecture

02:24:02.159 --> 02:24:04.800
where we split the application into three layers.

02:24:05.460 --> 02:24:07.600
We have an application interface layer

02:24:07.600 --> 02:24:10.600
that is responsible with managing the interface for users

02:24:10.600 --> 02:24:11.760
and external applications.

02:24:12.460 --> 02:24:14.680
So in addition to a graphical user interface,

02:24:15.260 --> 02:24:17.840
a web interface, a command line interface,

02:24:17.960 --> 02:24:19.700
it could also be an API as well,

02:24:19.819 --> 02:24:21.280
application programming interface.

02:24:22.440 --> 02:24:24.899
The processing layer then is responsible

02:24:24.899 --> 02:24:27.520
with containing the actual logic

02:24:27.520 --> 02:24:31.760
of how the application is implemented

02:24:31.760 --> 02:24:33.140
without specifying the data,

02:24:33.280 --> 02:24:35.780
and then the data layer contains that information.

02:24:35.780 --> 02:24:40.360
so this

02:24:40.360 --> 02:24:40.840
layering is

02:24:40.840 --> 02:24:41.159
found in

02:24:41.159 --> 02:24:41.380
distributed

02:24:41.380 --> 02:24:42.560
information

02:24:42.560 --> 02:24:43.100
systems

02:24:43.100 --> 02:24:43.500
especially

02:24:43.500 --> 02:24:44.020
the ones

02:24:44.020 --> 02:24:44.360
that use

02:24:44.360 --> 02:24:44.800
databases

02:24:44.800 --> 02:24:45.880
so

02:24:45.880 --> 02:24:46.500
whenever you

02:24:46.500 --> 02:24:47.200
hear about

02:24:47.200 --> 02:24:48.700
web applications

02:24:48.700 --> 02:24:49.260
they are

02:24:49.260 --> 02:24:49.560
typically

02:24:49.560 --> 02:24:50.140
implemented

02:24:50.140 --> 02:24:50.860
by following

02:24:50.860 --> 02:24:51.360
this three

02:24:51.360 --> 02:24:52.140
layered approach

02:24:52.140 --> 02:24:53.300
you might

02:24:53.300 --> 02:24:53.640
have heard

02:24:53.640 --> 02:24:54.020
already

02:24:54.020 --> 02:24:55.360
of a

02:24:55.360 --> 02:24:55.860
full stack

02:24:55.860 --> 02:24:56.320
developer

02:24:56.320 --> 02:24:57.180
front end

02:24:57.180 --> 02:24:57.640
back end

02:24:57.640 --> 02:24:58.079
developer

02:24:58.079 --> 02:24:58.920
so the

02:24:58.920 --> 02:24:59.460
front end

02:24:59.460 --> 02:24:59.800
is the

02:24:59.800 --> 02:25:00.120
equivalent

02:25:00.120 --> 02:25:00.460
of the

02:25:00.460 --> 02:25:00.900
application

02:25:00.900 --> 02:25:01.340
interface

02:25:01.340 --> 02:25:02.020
the back

02:25:02.020 --> 02:25:02.180
end

02:25:02.180 --> 02:25:02.420
is the

02:25:02.420 --> 02:25:02.720
equivalent

02:25:02.720 --> 02:25:03.020
of the

02:25:03.020 --> 02:25:03.440
processing

02:25:03.440 --> 02:25:04.280
layer

02:25:04.280 --> 02:25:04.680
and the

02:25:04.680 --> 02:25:05.280
database

02:25:05.280 --> 02:25:13.440
because usually websites are built with the database in mind, the database sits at the data layer or represents the data.

02:25:14.140 --> 02:25:16.620
Now, I just want to make a small parenthesis here.

02:25:17.220 --> 02:25:23.020
I'm sure you might also have heard about another architecture, but they represent something else.

02:25:23.180 --> 02:25:29.659
So you might have heard of the model view controller architecture, which is very popular in web development.

02:25:29.659 --> 02:25:40.799
So Model View Controller, where you implement your application by having a model component, a view component, a controller component.

02:25:41.459 --> 02:25:46.440
So this is how modern day websites or graphical user-based interfaces are implemented.

02:25:46.959 --> 02:25:54.680
And this architecture, it's not necessarily a layered architecture because it follows a cyclical, let's say, architecture.

02:25:54.860 --> 02:25:59.360
So the model view controller architecture tries to say that when you implement an application,

02:25:59.899 --> 02:26:02.760
the view part of the application, which will be the interface,

02:26:03.059 --> 02:26:06.120
will always be updated whenever the model gets updated.

02:26:06.680 --> 02:26:09.639
The user sees the view and then operates on the view

02:26:09.639 --> 02:26:13.200
to trigger functions on the controller part of the application

02:26:13.200 --> 02:26:16.659
and the controller part, the processing layer that does some sort of processing,

02:26:17.020 --> 02:26:18.520
and then modifies the data.

02:26:18.780 --> 02:26:22.139
So it modifies the model of the application, which then updates the view.

02:26:22.559 --> 02:26:25.239
So this is meant to represent the cycle of modifications

02:26:25.239 --> 02:26:26.779
inside your application.

02:26:26.899 --> 02:26:28.719
Again, a very popular architecture,

02:26:29.399 --> 02:26:31.399
but it's different than the application layer.

02:26:31.659 --> 02:26:33.699
But they're compatible with each other,

02:26:33.699 --> 02:26:35.819
so you can have both of them at the same time.

02:26:36.779 --> 02:26:40.079
Again, a very simple example would be with a search engine

02:26:40.079 --> 02:26:41.819
where the user interfaces were.

02:26:42.159 --> 02:26:44.219
You process the query, or anyways,

02:26:44.279 --> 02:26:46.399
you process the tokens that are being passed,

02:26:46.459 --> 02:26:48.519
you parse the tokens from the query,

02:26:48.879 --> 02:26:51.399
then you pass them to some sort of processing level server

02:26:51.399 --> 02:26:54.959
that generates the query, and then you ask a database,

02:26:55.680 --> 02:26:57.719
and then the database will give you some answers.

02:26:58.000 --> 02:27:02.340
You'll use some sort of ranking algorithm to create the results

02:27:02.340 --> 02:27:05.379
and send them back to the user interface that will then be presented to the user.

02:27:06.059 --> 02:27:07.579
Anyway, very simple to understand.

02:27:08.180 --> 02:27:12.840
So there's one main disadvantage of this layered architecture.

02:27:13.260 --> 02:27:15.859
So the main disadvantage is that it's very strict.

02:27:17.639 --> 02:27:19.779
Okay, so again, the layers are top.

02:27:19.780 --> 02:27:22.200
will depend on the layers on the bottom.

02:27:22.200 --> 02:27:25.200
It does not really allow you to have, let's say,

02:27:25.200 --> 02:27:28.000
more complex form of dependencies.

02:27:28.000 --> 02:27:33.659
And then what happens here is that if you think about the dependability

02:27:33.659 --> 02:27:36.800
goal of distributed systems, we say that the component depends

02:27:36.800 --> 02:27:38.680
on another component.

02:27:38.680 --> 02:27:41.880
And if we follow this layer architecture,

02:27:41.880 --> 02:27:47.280
it means that all of the layers, excluding the last layer,

02:27:47.280 --> 02:27:51.380
depend on the most bottom layer of the system. So it creates a strong

02:27:51.380 --> 02:27:55.760
dependency here. This is something that maybe we want to avoid sometimes in

02:27:55.760 --> 02:28:00.300
systems. Maybe we want to have more flexibility on how the dependency that

02:28:00.300 --> 02:28:07.700
say hierarchy gets created. So for this, people have explored a different class

02:28:07.700 --> 02:28:14.100
of logical architectures in distributed systems. Sometimes these class of

02:28:14.100 --> 02:28:18.979
of architectures are called object-based styles or service-oriented architectures.

02:28:19.399 --> 02:28:21.880
So now here, instead of creating these strict layers,

02:28:22.420 --> 02:28:25.120
what we do is we create various components, right,

02:28:25.140 --> 02:28:30.460
and we express the dependency by having components call functions of other components.

02:28:30.620 --> 02:28:33.700
So this looks a lot like object-oriented programming.

02:28:34.460 --> 02:28:38.300
So we can design a distributed system that is made out of multiple components,

02:28:38.540 --> 02:28:40.860
and then the components can make calls to other components,

02:28:40.940 --> 02:28:42.979
but they don't have to follow this strict hiring.

02:28:43.219 --> 02:28:44.379
So they're more fluid.

02:28:45.100 --> 02:28:49.359
This can be, of course, implemented, and that's why whenever you see this architecture,

02:28:50.000 --> 02:28:53.279
you typically see object-oriented programming designs as well there.

02:28:53.879 --> 02:28:59.100
Object-oriented programming works very well because it clearly separates, you know,

02:28:59.100 --> 02:29:04.359
the state and how you modify the state from the interface on how you access, let's say,

02:29:04.459 --> 02:29:07.399
a specific object or a specific service, right?

02:29:07.399 --> 02:29:14.719
So if you remember, one of the more important properties of object-oriented programming was encapsulation.

02:29:14.719 --> 02:29:26.340
So if we can build services that are also well encapsulated, where, again, modifications to the state can happen through very well-defined interfaces, then we will have very good systems.

02:29:26.340 --> 02:29:43.680
So you can have, of course, distributed systems where the services are organized as these objects or components that manage their own states and they can be accessed through different interfaces or through their custom interfaces and they can be accessed in a different way.

02:29:43.680 --> 02:29:52.220
Now, one of the main problems of implementing an object-based style architecture or a service

02:29:52.220 --> 02:29:58.300
variant is that all of the components that need to talk with another component, they

02:29:58.300 --> 02:30:02.300
need to know what are the details of the interface of the component that they want to talk.

02:30:03.000 --> 02:30:07.000
So imagine if these components are actually designed by different teams.

02:30:07.559 --> 02:30:11.220
They have one software engineering team that maybe works on one part of the application

02:30:11.219 --> 02:30:14.099
and then another team that works on another part, right?

02:30:14.519 --> 02:30:15.859
And they have to have now knowledge

02:30:15.859 --> 02:30:17.319
about each other's interfaces.

02:30:17.679 --> 02:30:20.420
And we know that sometimes interfaces change, right?

02:30:20.459 --> 02:30:22.019
Sometimes we need to update them.

02:30:22.119 --> 02:30:23.219
Sometimes we need to change them.

02:30:23.319 --> 02:30:24.479
Sometimes we add features,

02:30:24.699 --> 02:30:26.659
which means that if we change the interface

02:30:26.659 --> 02:30:28.899
of one of these object implementations,

02:30:28.899 --> 02:30:30.840
we have to let the other objects know

02:30:30.840 --> 02:30:33.019
that the interface has changed, right?

02:30:33.079 --> 02:30:35.799
So this creates a cascade of modifications in the system.

02:30:36.439 --> 02:30:37.920
So one of the challenges was, you know,

02:30:37.920 --> 02:30:42.780
can we implement some sort of general interface for service-oriented architectures?

02:30:43.159 --> 02:30:46.159
A way where it makes it very easy for us to change that code

02:30:46.159 --> 02:30:48.760
without having to recompile the entire program.

02:30:50.200 --> 02:30:54.159
So this gave birth to, and I'm sure you've heard this term,

02:30:54.540 --> 02:30:57.559
of REST APIs or REST architectures.

02:30:58.559 --> 02:31:03.680
So RESTful architectures are a type of service-oriented architecture

02:31:03.680 --> 02:31:06.180
that is, again, very popular, especially over the web.

02:31:06.180 --> 02:31:10.600
that has a very interesting way of organizing, let's say, resources

02:31:10.600 --> 02:31:13.159
and access to resources in a distributed system.

02:31:13.720 --> 02:31:17.120
So now, instead of creating these components that act as objects

02:31:17.120 --> 02:31:21.639
that have interfaces in state, people notice that what we need, actually,

02:31:21.760 --> 02:31:24.159
in a Cynid-distributed system, or what we want,

02:31:24.220 --> 02:31:28.860
is to just modify certain resources in it,

02:31:28.920 --> 02:31:30.920
certain data resources most of the time.

02:31:31.139 --> 02:31:34.559
In web applications, I'm just accessing a website,

02:31:34.559 --> 02:31:37.019
or I'm changing a row in the database.

02:31:37.199 --> 02:31:39.219
So I'm just accessing some resources there.

02:31:40.719 --> 02:31:44.879
So then they thought about another organization

02:31:44.879 --> 02:31:52.059
and REST stands for representational stage transfer.

02:31:52.859 --> 02:31:56.680
So this REST architecture follows the following,

02:31:57.180 --> 02:31:58.779
let's say, philosophy.

02:31:59.420 --> 02:32:01.639
So you view the distributed system

02:32:01.639 --> 02:32:03.119
as a collection of resources

02:32:03.120 --> 02:32:05.900
that are individually managed by different components,

02:32:06.440 --> 02:32:09.180
but we don't necessarily care about the details of the components,

02:32:09.720 --> 02:32:12.579
and the resources may be added, removed, retrieved,

02:32:12.579 --> 02:32:15.060
or modified by remote applications.

02:32:15.600 --> 02:32:17.660
There are, of course, a few properties here.

02:32:18.220 --> 02:32:20.300
To have a REST system, you have to make sure

02:32:20.300 --> 02:32:23.360
that the resources are identified through a single naming schema.

02:32:24.100 --> 02:32:27.040
So on each website, you need to have a unique naming schema

02:32:27.040 --> 02:32:29.180
to be able to identify each resource uniquely.

02:32:29.660 --> 02:32:31.400
You're not allowed to have and be viewed it.

02:32:33.120 --> 02:32:40.220
All of the services, all of the components, they offer the exact same interface for working or interacting with the resources.

02:32:40.900 --> 02:32:44.560
So you cannot change the way you call functions to add and remove.

02:32:44.900 --> 02:32:48.640
They have to be, again, the same interface across all services.

02:32:50.160 --> 02:32:54.300
The messages sent to or from a service are fully self-described.

02:32:54.840 --> 02:33:00.099
So when you send one of these operations or you call one of these interfaces, let's say,

02:33:00.439 --> 02:33:03.659
you include the details on how you want the operation to be calculated,

02:33:03.859 --> 02:33:07.219
but it's part of the message, not part of the interface.

02:33:08.179 --> 02:33:10.459
And then after executing an operation at a service,

02:33:10.840 --> 02:33:13.019
the component forgets everything about the car,

02:33:13.139 --> 02:33:15.579
so we don't store any state about it.

02:33:15.779 --> 02:33:19.719
So the basic operations that are supported in the rest architectures

02:33:19.719 --> 02:33:22.739
are, of course, the put, get, beat, and post operations.

02:33:22.739 --> 02:33:27.979
So, if you have ever implemented any kind of website, you probably have used these operations

02:33:27.979 --> 02:33:35.180
because website technology relies actually on, let's say, a REST architecture or a RESTful

02:33:35.180 --> 02:33:35.579
architecture.

02:33:36.180 --> 02:33:39.260
So, to create a new resource, you'll need to use the PUT function.

02:33:39.420 --> 02:33:43.739
So, these are the name of the operations or of the functions that are available as part

02:33:43.739 --> 02:33:45.420
of the interface of all of the services.

02:33:45.639 --> 02:33:48.260
So, you can create a new resource using the PUT operation.

02:33:48.260 --> 02:33:53.200
You can retrieve the state of a resource by using get, delete a resource by using delete,

02:33:53.340 --> 02:33:55.420
and then modify a resource by using posts.

02:33:56.780 --> 02:34:02.540
So let's see some examples of differences if we were to implement the same distributed

02:34:02.540 --> 02:34:07.380
system by using a REST interface versus using an object-oriented interface.

02:34:07.940 --> 02:34:13.280
So let's say that we are Amazon and we want to implement a way for people to be able to

02:34:13.280 --> 02:34:16.920
store data under the form of objects, right?

02:34:16.920 --> 02:34:25.799
This is called Amazon Simple Storage Service, or S3, that you can go and access today, and you can pay money to be able to store objects in their cloud.

02:34:26.819 --> 02:34:30.520
Another example of a system that does the same thing is, of course, Dropbox.

02:34:31.200 --> 02:34:34.540
A very interesting side note here, historical side note.

02:34:35.100 --> 02:34:44.600
In the early days of Dropbox, because storage was so cheap, actually Dropbox was just a web interface that ran on top of Amazon S3.

02:34:44.600 --> 02:34:50.680
So they store everything in Amazon and just provide some web interface to do some additional functionality.

02:34:50.680 --> 02:34:55.760
Until it became infeasible for them to use S3 because S3 became more expensive.

02:34:55.760 --> 02:34:57.819
So then they build their own system.

02:34:57.819 --> 02:35:00.159
But at the beginning, they just ran a wrapper on top of S3.

02:35:00.159 --> 02:35:02.000
That looked nice.

02:35:02.000 --> 02:35:05.760
One quick way to make cheap money with it, right?

02:35:05.760 --> 02:35:08.700
Alright, so the way Amazon S3 works...

02:35:08.700 --> 02:35:14.520
It allows you to store objects that need to be placed inside the bot.

02:35:14.520 --> 02:35:19.319
You can think about these objects kind of equivalent of files and buckets the equivalent

02:35:19.319 --> 02:35:20.319
of directories.

02:35:20.319 --> 02:35:23.239
The only difference is that you cannot have buckets inside buckets.

02:35:23.239 --> 02:35:27.840
OK, so you can have directories inside directories in a file system, but not here.

02:35:27.840 --> 02:35:31.819
So you can only have buckets and then objects inside buckets.

02:35:31.819 --> 02:35:36.640
So then what Amazon did in order to implement the REST interface, is of course, they need

02:35:36.640 --> 02:35:41.399
to provide a unique way to identify all of the resources in their system.

02:35:41.399 --> 02:35:51.379
So what they did to uniquely identify the resource is they used actually the URL that you punched in in your, let's say, web browser, or punched in into your program.

02:35:51.899 --> 02:35:57.039
They used the URL information to actually determine the unique location of an object.

02:35:57.040 --> 02:36:02.720
So the way this works, they use a subdomain for the bucket name.

02:36:03.360 --> 02:36:07.880
They include it underneath S3 and AmazonAWL.com and then slash.

02:36:08.020 --> 02:36:11.740
And then here they include the object identifier or object name.

02:36:12.520 --> 02:36:16.860
Okay, so this is their unique way of identifying objects inside S3.

02:36:17.340 --> 02:36:23.460
So now you can use those typical REST operations to actually interact with the objects or with the buckets.

02:36:24.119 --> 02:36:31.459
So you can create buckets by doing a put operation along with the URL.

02:36:32.099 --> 02:36:38.099
So if you want to create a new bucket, let's say, you do put http colon slash slash the name of my bucket,

02:36:38.619 --> 02:36:46.299
which will be my, let's say, video game screenshots, .s3, .amazonaws.com.

02:36:46.299 --> 02:36:50.259
if I want to list the objects in a bucket

02:36:50.259 --> 02:36:53.379
I will do a get operation over HTTP

02:36:53.379 --> 02:36:56.259
let's say video game screenshots

02:36:56.259 --> 02:36:58.479
on Amazon AWS.com

02:36:58.479 --> 02:37:01.599
and then the service implementing these functions

02:37:01.599 --> 02:37:03.699
will know how to return those values

02:37:03.699 --> 02:37:05.039
if I want to create an object

02:37:05.039 --> 02:37:06.679
I will do the same foot command

02:37:06.679 --> 02:37:08.899
the same URL but I would add slash

02:37:08.899 --> 02:37:09.979
and then the name of the object

02:37:09.979 --> 02:37:11.340
that I want to include there

02:37:11.340 --> 02:37:12.899
keep in mind that in S3

02:37:12.899 --> 02:37:16.139
the objects are considered to be blobs of data

02:37:16.139 --> 02:37:24.859
So Amazon S3 does not do any kind of processing to distinguish between binary data and human readable data.

02:37:25.019 --> 02:37:26.879
Okay, everything is binary for it.

02:37:27.420 --> 02:37:32.279
Okay, so again, this would be, if we want to, again, to use REST, right,

02:37:32.599 --> 02:37:39.139
this is how Amazon S3 organizes resources, and those are the functions that can be used to interact with that resource.

02:37:39.319 --> 02:37:44.959
It's very easy, you can use the same approach for implementing services inside a distributed system of your choice.

02:37:46.139 --> 02:37:50.479
But Amazon S3 also provides an object-oriented interface.

02:37:50.479 --> 02:37:54.439
In this context, it's called a SOAP interface or some sort of API.

02:37:55.319 --> 02:38:00.219
They allow you to use Python, for example, and interact with the services using a Python API.

02:38:01.039 --> 02:38:09.519
Now, the problem here, or not a problem, but in order to interact with all of the resources provided through this API,

02:38:10.180 --> 02:38:13.459
Amazon had to provide actually a bunch of functions.

02:38:14.159 --> 02:38:16.139
So this is very different than the rest interface.

02:38:16.379 --> 02:38:18.779
So now in order to create the buckets, to list the buckets,

02:38:18.859 --> 02:38:21.000
I would have separate functions that need to be implemented,

02:38:21.779 --> 02:38:25.279
which means that now when we write a program, we have a choice.

02:38:25.500 --> 02:38:28.879
So we can write a program using the object-oriented interface.

02:38:29.539 --> 02:38:35.419
So we import, for example, whatever packages Amazon has implemented in Python,

02:38:36.059 --> 02:38:39.599
and then we import the proper packet, and then we can call bucket create,

02:38:40.359 --> 02:38:42.619
bucket list, and all of the other operations.

